  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts with the full portal scene. It passes the typecheck. I haven't run `vite build` or loaded it in a browser, so I haven't seen how it looks or checked the frame rate.\n\n- **Portal:** an 18-block stone ring with glowing rune accents stands on a two-tier stone platform. A custom GLSL vortex disc fills the ring. It has cyan and violet spiral arms, a bright core and a glowing rim, animated through a time uniform I update each frame.\n- **Surroundings:** eight ruined pillars, rubble, a fallen column, a dark ground plane and exponential fog form the dusk haze. A dim violet hemisphere light and a warm orange directional light with shadows provide the dusk lighting, and bloom provides the glow.\n- **Particles:** 260 cyan and violet spheres spiral into the portal, and 70 motes rise from the platform, sway and fade. All are instanced emissive spheres updated on the CPU every frame. Dead particles respawn, so the live count stays at 330.\n- **Lighting:** two cyan/violet point lights and a low violet one pulse slowly and light the stones and platform.\n- **Camera:** it orbits slowly. Because the orbit is a full circle, the view will pass behind the portal. The disc is double-sided, so it still renders from there.\n- **Contract:** `window.__TASK_STATE__` is updated every frame, and `__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()`.\n\nI'm unsure whether the engine's bloom reacts to the vortex and particle brightness as strongly as I intended. That depends on where bloom sits relative to tone mapping.",
